Corrected native memlat checks (Azyr review A01-027--030)
======================================================

From the source root:

    python3 tools/testing/selftests/memlat/test_memlat.py --self-test-negatives
    python3 tools/testing/selftests/memlat/test_memlat.py \
        --cc /path/to/clang-r614150/bin/clang --self-test-negatives

Requires Python 3, git, a host C compiler and its undefined-behavior sanitizer
runtime. Does not download anything, modify kernel sources, build a kernel or
run hardware tests. Core dumps are disabled for intentional assertion failures.

The script checks the three-source patch/inverse and SHA256 provenance, and
compares all three original ARMv8 assembly-worker bodies and seven legacy
workers against the reconstructed preimages. It compiles actual extracted
monitor functions, structs, initialization and SCM C wrappers against mock
kernel/perf/SMP primitives; model.c is not a second driver implementation.
The mock waits exercise deferred reverse-order and completion-before-wait
callbacks. They check callback draining, lock scope, sparse CPU ids across
32/64-bit boundaries, per-event read failures, dispatch errors, preserved
baselines/data during SCM/error skips, idle/isolated no-dispatch and vote clearing,
offline/re-online epochs, zero elapsed time/zero
cycles, optional stall counters, nested SCM accounting/argument forwarding,
startup allocation failures, repeat starts and busy-SCM teardown. Extracted
stall arithmetic is compared with Python's unbounded integer reference on
20,484 deterministic cases. Seven source mutants compile successfully and must
fail model assertions: omitted drain, skipped busy cleanup, partial publication,
omitted startup unwind, omitted CPU-hotplug read lock, waking observed idle CPUs
and dispatching to isolated CPUs.

These sequential mocks DO NOT prove real SMP memory ordering, CPU hotplug/perf
scheduling races, interrupt/SMC behavior, suspend, device stability, throughput,
latency or power. Native AArch64 object code-generation evidence and limitations
are recorded separately in:

    Documentation/dev-tools/azyr-sm6250-memlat-native-validation.json
    Documentation/dev-tools/azyr-sm6250-memlat-native-validation.txt
    Documentation/dev-tools/azyr-sm6250-review-03.md

This adaptation targets the shipped built-in miatoll monitor. Module linkage is
not supported/proven by these tests: perf_event_read_local is not exported in
this tree (the old governor registration helpers are also unexported). No
private SMP or perf symbol was exported to get a module build through.
